$DIR/main.p:10:6: v is declared before a, but it holds a view of a (vec.New[int](&a), line 12) that its drop uses, and a is dropped first
    Variables are dropped in reverse order of declaration, so a is dropped
    before v. Dropping v reads or releases what it holds, and the view made by
    vec.New[int](&a) at line 12 would then look at memory a has freed. A
    variable whose drop releases or writes through a view of another local
    variable must be declared after that variable (section 18.3).
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Declare v after a
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)
